// @trap
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ff64` is inferred as `f64`
// An `x as! u8` TRAPS on a fraction as well as on a range miss: the test is "integral AND in
// range", the same two questions `as! i32` asks, so `3.9 as! u8` is refused for the reason
// `3.9 as! i32` is even though 3 would fit a byte comfortably (D1587).
//
// This is also the fixture that pins the PEEPHOLE out of the `u8` domain. `trunc(d) as! i32`
// skips the exactness test because `i32.trunc_f64_s` already traps on i32's range — an
// argument that is FALSE at 0..255, where the conversion instruction enforces nothing. The
// program below does not spell `trunc`, and `as-cast-u8-trio.vl`'s in-range rows are what
// prove the test that replaces it still admits a good value.
function ff64(x: f64): f64 { x }
const frac = ff64(3.9)
print(frac as! u8)
print(-1)
